## Why This Is Asked
This evaluates your ability to design a scalable drift-resilient Terraform workflow across multiple AWS accounts while ensuring safe automation and auditable infrastructure changes.

## Key Concepts
- Drift detection, automatic remediation, policy enforcement (Sentinel/OPA)
- Environment-specific remote backends, provider aliasing, central baseline state
- Module architecture boundaries, testing frameworks (Kitchen-Terraform/Terratest), CI/CD gates

## Code Example
```hcl
provider "aws" {
  alias  = "dev"
  region = "us-west-2"
}
terraform {
  required_providers {
    aws = {
      source  = "hashicorp/aws"
      version = "~> 5.0"
    }
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This tests cross-cloud module design, provider aliasing, and policy-driven governance at scale.

## Key Concepts
- Terraform multi-provider modules
- Provider aliasing and per-tenant scoping
- Open Policy Agent integration with terraform show -json plan
- CI gates and fail-fast

## Code Example
```javascript
// Module interface skeleton (Terraform-like)
module data_lake {
  source = ./modules/data_lake
  tenants = {
    t1 = { cloud = aws, region = us-east-1 }
    t2 = { cloud = gcp, region = us-central1 }
  }
  quotas = {
    t1 = { data_lake_size_gb = 100 }
    t2 = { data_lake_size_gb = 200 }
  }
}
```

```javascript
// Sample OPA policy skeleton
package terraform.policy
default allow = false
allow {
  input.resource_changes[_].change.actions[_] == create
}
```

